; 25-parse.kurt

; `parse` is `tokenize`'s (24-tokenize.kurt) sibling one stage further: it
; shows the actual tree-structured term an expression parses into (as an
; s-expression), plus the syntax declarations of every symbol involved.  The
; term is shown in the normal form Kurt stores and matches: a `flat` operator
; flattened, the arguments of a `sym` one sorted (`parse c + 1 + c` gives
; `(+ 1 c c)` with numbers.kurt).  It's
; the fastest way to check that an operator you just declared binds the way
; you think it does -- the lessons on declaring syntax (27-infix.kurt to
; 35-flat.kurt) lean on it heavily.

bool A, B
parse A implies B
